      "lineNbr": 4757,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-22T22:57:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To make it clear for the callers what combinations are legal/illegal, we could do:\n\n    struct ec_params_hang_detect {\n        uint16_t arg;     /* command argument */\n        uint16_t command; /* STOP, START, STATUS, FLAG */\n        uint16_t reserved;\n        uint16_t reboot_timeout_sec;\n    }\n\n* STOP(\u003d0x8000) and START(\u003d0x4000) don\u0027t take argument.\n* STATUS(\u003d0x0001) command takes:\n  arg\u003d0: Clear status\n  arg\u003d1: Get status\n* FLAG(\u003d0x0000) command takes:\n  BIT(3): START_ON_RESUME\n  BIT(10): STOP_ON_SUSPEND\n\nThe command values are chosen to parse the old ec_params_hang_detect.flags using the same code. I\u0027m not sure whether we care about mixing old \u0026 new FW \u0026 OS, though.",
      "parentUuid": "7a0d964e_cf25e370",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4738,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 4757,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "03d61b6644ea1ceb8a4dcf549f1cb456237e0f60",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5289f1ca_cbe1ef51",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 4757,
      "author": {
        "id": 1442602
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-24T12:03:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So here is the thing - I have left the structure binary layout to not change the ABI (also all the flags leaving as they are and just marking OBSOLETE). I\u0027m not aware of any usage of this code, but keep in mind that this part is also copied to the kernel. Earlier we\u0027ve talked with Keith to keep the ABI and just \"obsolete\" unused parts...",
      "parentUuid": "7a3519d7_dfb3e5cf",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4738,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 4757,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "03d61b6644ea1ceb8a4dcf549f1cb456237e0f60",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ebcaac1b_82dbb3e1",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 4757,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-24T19:03:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e  I\u0027m not aware of any usage of this code, \n\nI also don\u0027t see any host-side code calling this command or the \u0027ectool hangdetect\u0027 command. So, I think it\u0027s safe to change the interface. (That makes sense since this feature has been unused for years?) Is daisydog already petting the EC? I don\u0027t see \u0027struct ec_params_hang_detect\u0027 being used in the kernel. So, I assume daisydog change will follow.\n\nOnce we enable this feature, it\u0027s going to be hard to change the interface because we would need to take care of backward compatibility.\n\nSo, I believe it\u0027s a good time to redefine a clean interface. I don\u0027t see a point of inheriting a not-ideal unused interface and being handcuffed.\n\nBTW, you can use union to keep ectool compatible:\n\n    union {\n        uint32_t flags;\n        struct {\n            uint16_t args;\n            uint16_t command;\n        };\n    };\n\nor update ectool together.",
      "parentUuid": "5289f1ca_cbe1ef51",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4738,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 4757,
        "endChar": 32
